## Context

The author wants adversarial smoke testing of the plugin, not just the
happy-path DOM/style smoke that already exists (per ticket 0686's own
note: "the existing DOM/style smoke does not establish keyboard or
screen-reader behavior" -- the same gap applies to the scheduler's
failure handling). This is the raison d'etre theme: the sitter is a
supervisor, so its resilience to its own dependencies misbehaving is the
thing worth testing, more than its happy path.

Each scenario asserts against the journal from 0689 (the ring buffer /
`Zotero.debug()` stream), not against UI state -- so these tests inherit
0689's trustworthiness rather than duplicating a second assertion surface.

## Scenarios (drive the scheduler and bootstrap seams with hostile fakes)

| Scenario | Fake | Expected |
|---|---|---|
| Worker promise never settles | `ensure()` pends forever | No 2nd submission; heartbeat continues; no hang verdict rendered |
| Worker rejects mid-job | rejects after 3 progress ticks | Suppressed by identity; next doc admitted; error line logged |
| Worker resolves, pack absent/corrupt | `inspect` finds nothing | Not counted as coverage; "finished with exception" |
| RAM/disk reading unavailable | stat throws | Admission refused; refusal logged; no busy loop |
| Disk crosses threshold mid-job | 9 GiB -> 2 GiB | Current job continues (check, not cap); next admission refused |
| Cache file corrupt / partial line | crafted cache | Falls back to inspection; drops bad rows; counts them |
| Cache directory unwritable | write throws | One log line; estimates still computed in memory |
| Dialog closed & reopened mid-job | open/close/open | One instance, no duplicated listeners, state preserved |
| Two windows, two `startup()` calls | double call | One global object; one active submission |
| Disable during active job | `shutdown` while pending | `shutdown(disable)` last; no further `submit` |
| Quit during cache write | reject write chain at quit | No partial line, or dropped on next load |
| Clock jump backwards | `Date.now` steps back 1h | Elapsed uses monotonic time; finish shows "unavailable" |
| Empirical upper bound exceeded | duration > p95 | Finish time "unavailable, not now" |

Worker-process death itself (kill the worker, not just reject a promise)
is out of scope here -- that is ticket 0681's crash-recovery detection.
These tests only assert that 0681's absence degrades to "no further
submissions," never to a retry loop.

## Acceptance

- [ ] Each row is one test, shown red once against a deliberately broken
      scheduler -- record the red commit hash in this ticket's log.
- [ ] No test uses sleep-based timing; the scheduler takes an injectable
      clock (confirm `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` already supports
      this, or add it).
- [ ] Tests run from `make check`'s integration tier, not only by hand.
